[{"role": "system", "content": "You maintain `skill.md`, a short, general reference of design lessons for an automated process that's evolving a population of solitaire game variants (written in a custom format called SGDL) generation by generation.\n\nEach generation, many independently-running variants are each given exactly one focused revision based on their own simulated playtest results, by a separate process that has no visibility into what any of the other variants are doing. Whoever makes each revision also writes a short \"insight\": a general, transferable lesson suggested by that one specific change and its outcome, not a narration of what they did. You'll be given the current skill.md (or told it doesn't exist yet, if this is the first generation) plus the full batch of insights produced this generation, and your job is to produce the next version of skill.md.\n\nTreat skill.md as a living document you rewrite each time, not a log you append to:\n\n- Integrate new insights into the existing structure rather than tacking them on. If several insights from this generation (or an insight and an existing point) are really saying the same thing in different words, merge them into one clearer, possibly stronger statement -- don't keep near-duplicates as separate bullets.\n- If this generation's evidence contradicts or weakens an existing point, revise or remove it rather than letting both versions stand.\n- Look for what's well-supported. If most of this generation's relevant insights agree, that's worth keeping or strengthening; if only one or two insights suggest something that doesn't fit with everything else, it may just be noise from one particular variant rather than a real general lesson, and it's fine to leave out.\n- Write every point as a general principle about solitaire design, not as a fact about one specific file's exact numbers (\"foundation piles that no move rule ever targets sit permanently empty regardless of other changes\" is useful; \"Trizug's draw pile of 24 cards was too small\" is not, since nobody will recognize \"Trizug\" later).\n- Organize the document under a small number of clear headers (e.g. draw pile design, difficulty and winnability, avoiding unused elements, win conditions), and keep the whole thing short enough that it's actually useful as a quick reference -- a sprawling document nobody can read in full defeats the purpose. A specific cap on total bullet points is given to you below; treat it as a hard limit, not a target to approach.\n- It's completely fine for this to be a light touch-up most generations and a more substantial rewrite occasionally, depending on what the evidence actually supports.\n\nEnd your response with the complete new skill.md -- the whole document, not a diff -- in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response. You can reason about what to keep, merge, or change beforehand.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Current skill.md:\n\n```skill\n# skill.md\n\n## Establish a valid, playable core\n- Treat compile failures, bad deck accounting, invalid references, impossible predicates, and overlapping move-definition conflicts as first-order design failures. Fix the smallest structural issue that restores a runnable game before tuning difficulty.\n- Prefer minimal, informative repairs: preserve existing pile roles, card flow, and move graph where possible; restore exact card totals; and re-check that every rule, sink, and win-condition target is valid in context.\n- Validate rule/setup compatibility early. Build and sink predicates must match the actual deck structure: reduced suits, duplicated suits, multiple deck copies, prefilled receiving piles, or collapsed color distributions can make standard-looking tableau or foundation rules impossible, trivial, or too narrow.\n- Repair opening contact before balancing challenge. Universal 0-move starts, tiny exhausted searches, or populated pile types with no legal role usually mean the opening layout or move graph lacks a reachable first progress route.\n\n## Align sinks, victory, and deck structure\n- If the win condition requires active piles to empty, the design needs at least one reachable, irreversible sink that the objective explicitly rewards. Rearrangement alone cannot satisfy an emptying goal.\n- Sink capacity and parallelism must match the deck\u2019s real completion structure. Duplicated suits, repeated suit-runs, multiple deck copies, reduced-suit decks, or partially occupied receiving piles can make a familiar foundation system mathematically unable to absorb the full deck.\n- Match the sink rule to the deck, not to convention. When standard same-suit ace-up foundations underfit the card set, adjust sink count, acceptance classes, or progression pattern so ordinary exposed cards can become durable progress.\n- Prefer incremental sinks over all-at-once completion gates. Stepwise advancement usually converts local organization into steady wins more reliably than requiring fully assembled runs before any irreversible progress is scored.\n- When a one-way receiving or accumulation area already exists, first consider making it the main victory sink, expanding its capacity, or aligning the win condition to it before adding new subsystems or more tableau freedom.\n- If a pile can receive cards but not meaningfully release them, it should either represent rewarded completion or be removed/reworked. One-way piles outside the win path often create dead sinks, blockers, or unwinnable bookkeeping goals.\n- Keep main progress sinks as irreversible as possible. Allow backflow only for a specific strategic purpose; otherwise it usually increases churn more than it adds meaningful decisions.\n\n## Shape mobility to support progress\n- Distinguish deadness from churn. Near-zero use and tiny exhausted searches usually indicate missing opening contact or an unreachable sink; high move counts, broad usage, and search-cap losses usually indicate reversible motion without enough rewarded progress.\n- When movement is plentiful but wins are absent, improve the sink before adding mobility: make progress reachable, incremental, and large enough to absorb the deck under the actual rules.\n- Favor movement rules that organize cards in the same direction the sink rewards. Tableau builds and transfers should prepare ordinary exposed cards for advancement, not create free circulation disconnected from victory.\n- Constrain the broadest reversible routes before adding new ones. Unrestricted empty-space fills, overly permissive helper piles, and generic single-card shuffling are common sources of search churn.\n- Prefer constrained empty-pile rules over unrestricted ones. Rank-gated, single-card, or otherwise narrow access to empties often rescues dead starts while preserving planning value and reducing solver noise.\n- Broad holding areas are risky. Helper piles should be clearly temporary and clearable, or else converted into structured progress zones.\n\n## Tune openings and pacing with small changes\n- For frequent 0- or 1-move losses in an otherwise coherent game, first improve opening contact with the smallest compatible change: slightly more starting exposure, a narrowly available workspace destination, or a modest increase in effective tableau capacity.\n- If some deals already win cleanly but others die almost immediately, suspect blocked information or cramped early workspace before rewriting the core build rules.\n- When a stock or deal pile feeds cards into active piles, it should participate in the main progress route rather than act as delayed blockage. Pacing elements work best when they expose or supply cards that can realistically reach the sink.\n\n## Express rules cleanly\n- Keep move definitions compact and non-overlapping. If the engine forbids multiple rules for the same source/destination pair, merge them into one coherent condition tree instead of scattering exceptions.\n```\n\nThis generation produced 100 insight(s) from independently revised variants:\n\n1. When a solitaire shows high move counts and broad pile usage but no wins, the usual problem is not mobility but lack of a practical irreversible sink. Reworking an existing receiving pile into a simple, always-reachable progress sink can convert churn into completion without rewriting the whole tableau.\n2. When a game shows high move counts and broad pile usage but never wins, the usual problem is not lack of mobility but lack of a mathematically adequate sink. Before loosening tableau rules, check that the receiving piles can actually absorb the whole deck under the real suit/copy structure and that the win condition rewards moving cards into them.\n3. When a game has plenty of movement but no wins, first check whether any destination actually counts as durable progress. A one-way receiving pile should usually be the victory sink; otherwise it often becomes either dead storage or a blocker that makes the empty-all objective impossible.\n4. When a solitaire uses a one-way accumulation pile, the win condition should usually track progress into that sink rather than require unrelated source piles to empty. Also, special draw piles are not always valid targets in every rule context, so compile-safe victory conditions should reference ordinary pile types that actually represent completion.\n5. When a solitaire has an \"empty these piles\" win condition, it needs a destination that can absorb cards irreversibly under ordinary positions. If all receiving piles follow foundation-style progression, duplicated suits or multiple deck copies can make the objective mathematically impossible even when moves exist.\n6. When a solitaire already has a one-way receiving area, making that area the explicit victory sink is often a better fix than adding more tableau freedom. Also, sink capacity must match the deck\u2019s actual completion structure: duplicated suits or multiple deck copies often need more foundation piles, not just looser movement.\n7. When a solitaire already wins consistently with full component usage but requires very long solution sequences, the likely problem is excess reversible mobility rather than lack of progress. Tightening access to empty spaces is often a strong first lever: it reduces churn while keeping the existing sink and card flow intact.\n8. When a design shows high search churn but no wins, first check whether its irreversible sinks can actually absorb the full deck under the real card set. A common repair is to promote an existing one-way receiving area into the primary win sink and size its parallel capacity to the deck\u2019s duplicated suits/copies.\n9. When a solitaire already wins often with full card/pile usage but still has a few fully-exhausted dead deals, prefer a small increase in starting contact or workspace over broadening move rules. A single extra exposed card or slot can eliminate structural dead starts without collapsing the game into trivial free circulation.\n10. When a solitaire has lots of legal rearrangement but no wins, first check whether the irreversible sink matches the deck\u2019s actual duplication pattern. A smaller number of foundations can be better than a larger conventional set if each pile then has enough capacity to absorb the repeated suit structure cleanly.\n11. When a solitaire shows high search-cap losses with moderate card usage, suspect reversible motion without enough rewarded progress. In multi-copy or reduced-suit decks, foundation capacity must match the number of full suit sequences in the deck; otherwise the win condition can be structurally underfit even if the rules look conventional.\n12. When a game\u2019s objective is to empty active piles, first verify that its irreversible sink can mathematically absorb the entire deck under the actual suit/deck structure. If many deals die at move 0, a small empty-space rule is often a better repair than broadly loosening every build rule.\n13. When many deals fail after only a handful of explored moves, improve opening contact before broadening the whole game: a narrowly gated use of empty piles can create early mobility without turning the tableau into unrestricted churn. Align single-card and stack empty-space rules as well; inconsistent empty-fill permissions often create accidental dead ends.\n14. When a solitaire already shows high move counts and moderate-to-high card usage on losses, the problem is often reversible churn rather than lack of mobility. Narrowing access to empty tableau spaces is a strong first lever because it reduces search noise while preserving the tactical value of creating vacancies.\n15. When a solitaire uses duplicated suits or multiple deck copies, first verify that the irreversible sink can absorb the entire deck under its own progression rule; otherwise no amount of tableau tuning will fix fundamental unwinnability.\n\nIf many losses show high search churn while some starts have zero moves, the design often needs both a reachable sink and a more natural organizing build rule, not just additional freedom to reshuffle cards.\n16. When a solitaire uses conventional ace-up same-suit foundations with multiple deck copies, foundation count must scale to the number of complete suit sequences in the deck. If the sink can\u2019t absorb the whole deck, added mobility tends to produce search churn rather than meaningful progress.\n17. When a solitaire has lots of legal rearrangement but no wins, first examine whether its irreversible sink can actually absorb the deck under the real card multiplicities. Also, if a helper area mainly accepts cards without clearly contributing to victory, tightening or removing access to it often reduces churn better than adding still more mobility.\n18. When a solitaire repeatedly exhausts after only a handful of moves with near-zero card usage, first check whether the victory sink can mathematically absorb the whole deck under the actual rules. A one-way receiving pile should either be the clear completion target or be removed; otherwise the game often becomes provably unwinnable before strategy even matters.\n19. When losses include both early proven unwinnability and long search churn, check whether the game\u2019s irreversible sinks can actually absorb the full deck under the real card multiset. A simple, always-available sink often improves a design more than adding more tableau mobility, because it turns local exposure into durable progress instead of endless rearrangement.\n20. When a solitaire already shows high card and pile usage but still loses mostly at search limits, the problem is often not lack of mobility but lack of a forgiving irreversible sink. Improving the sink so ordinary exposed cards can score progress usually helps more than adding even more tableau movement.\n21. When a solitaire shows high search-cap losses with moderate pile usage, the problem is often not lack of mobility but a sink that is too narrow or fragmented for the actual deck. In multi-copy or reduced-suit decks, increasing the number and accessibility of irreversible progress piles can help far more than adding more tableau movement.\n22. When a solitaire shows high move counts and decent card usage but no wins, the bottleneck is often not mobility but lack of incremental scoring. Converting a \u201ccomplete-run only\u201d sink into a stepwise irreversible sink can turn tableau organization into real progress without making the movement rules much looser.\n23. When a reduced-suit deck changes color structure, standard tableau predicates like alternate-color or same-color can collapse into something much narrower than intended; re-check what those predicates actually mean with the chosen suits. Also, if victory requires emptying play piles, the receiving foundations must have enough parallel capacity to absorb the entire deck under their progression rule.\n24. When a solitaire repeatedly exhausts without wins, first check whether its victory sink is both mathematically compatible with the deck and practically reachable from typical openings. A smaller, more numerous sink often improves solvability more cleanly than adding extra tableau movement, because it turns ordinary exposure into irreversible progress instead of more reversible churn.\n25. When a design shows high search-cap losses with broad pile usage, suspect reversible churn rather than lack of mobility; improving or repurposing an existing destination into a true progress sink is often more effective than adding yet more movement. Also, if a pile type behaves like a one-way accumulator, either make it part of the victory path or avoid letting it absorb cards that the win condition still expects elsewhere.\n26. When a solitaire file fails basic deck accounting, make the smallest count correction that preserves existing pile roles before changing rules. Restoring missing cards to an active, movable pile is usually more informative than pre-filling sinks, because it lets playtesting evaluate the intended card flow instead of a distorted shortcut state.\n27. When a solitaire uses repeated suit sets or multiple deck copies, foundation capacity must scale to the number of full suit sequences present. If the sink cannot mathematically absorb the deck, added mobility mostly creates search churn rather than meaningful progress.\n28. When a solitaire variant fails to compile, make the smallest fix that restores a runnable core before tuning difficulty. Also, align the win condition with the actual irreversible sink: if cards can only leave play by moving into a one-way pile, victory should usually reward filling that sink or emptying the source piles, not the sink itself.\n29. When some shuffled deals already win using the full deck but other deals die in only a few explored moves, the bottleneck is often opening exposure rather than the overall objective. A small increase in initial visible information can improve fairness and decision density without making the core game trivial.\n30. When a solver explores thousands of moves without finding wins, the problem is often excessive reversible mobility rather than insufficient legal moves. Tightening the broadest low-commitment route\u2014especially unrestricted access to empty tableau spaces\u2014can improve decision quality without rewriting the whole game.\n31. When a game loses in 0\u20131 moves across many deals, first fix opening visibility or contact before rewriting the move system. A sink can be theoretically correct and still be practically unreachable if too few cards are exposed to start the first irreversible moves.\n32. When some deals win fully but many others die after only a handful of moves, the problem is often blocked opening information rather than insufficient overall mobility. A small increase in starting exposure can improve solvability more cleanly than broadening move rules or adding more reversible workspace.\n33. When an engine treats source/destination pile pairs as unique rule slots, special cases for the same route should be merged into one condition tree rather than split across multiple moves. Fixing structural validity first is often the highest-leverage change, because balance data is meaningless until the game can actually run.\n34. When a solitaire design fails before playtesting, fix exact card accounting first and preserve the intended pile roles if possible. A minimal compile repair is more informative than a broader redesign, because it lets later evaluation reveal whether the core flow is actually functional.\n35. When a game requires tableau piles to empty, first verify that the irreversible sinks can actually absorb the entire deck under the real suit/deck mix. A familiar foundation count can become mathematically insufficient in reduced-suit or duplicated-suit decks, producing proven unwinnability no amount of tableau tuning will fix.\n36. When a solitaire has lots of legal rearrangement but no wins, check whether its irreversible sinks can actually absorb enough of the deck to satisfy the win condition. A small change that turns already-supported local patterns into stepwise discard progress often helps more than adding even more tableau mobility.\n37. When a solitaire file fails deck accounting, fix that exact mismatch before tuning difficulty: a minimal count correction preserves the current design so later playtest results are actually informative.\n\nWin conditions should express real goals, not invariants. A clause that is always true adds noise and can hide whether the game has a genuine completion path.\n38. When most losses are fully exhausted and some starts have no legal moves, first check whether the victory sink can mathematically absorb the whole deck under the actual card multiplicities. A tableau can be perfectly mobile yet still be unwinnable if the foundations under-capacity the deck.\n39. When many deals die immediately with near-zero card usage, the first repair is often not a stronger sink but a little more workspace. A single empty active pile can be enough\u2014but only if the empty-fill rule actually lets ordinary cards use it, rather than reserving it for rare ranks.\n40. When a solitaire has abundant legal moves but only search-cap losses, the usual problem is churn, not lack of mobility. Converting an existing receiving area into an irreversible, incremental sink that matches the deck\u2019s completion structure often improves solvability more effectively than adding yet more tableau movement.\n41. When a variant fails before simulation, fix the smallest structural defect first and preserve the existing move graph. Early playtest data is only meaningful once the deck total, sink capacity, and pile references are all valid.\n42. When a variant fails before play due to missing cards, prefer the smallest repair that preserves the intended roles of piles and rules; restoring exact deck accounting is more informative than redesigning mechanics before the game can even run.\n43. When a solitaire file fails validation, check deck arithmetic before redesigning rules: many \u201cbroken game\u201d symptoms come from a one-pile count mismatch, and the best first repair is usually the smallest change that restores exact card totals while preserving the game\u2019s intended structure.\n44. When a solitaire has abundant rearrangement but no wins, first check whether it has a sink that can absorb the whole deck in small, ordinary steps. A one-way ace-up foundation often works better than requiring tableau piles themselves to empty through pure reordering.\n45. When losses are fully exhausted after only a handful of moves, first check for a mathematical mismatch between sink capacity and the win condition. A familiar foundation rule can still make a game impossible if victory tracks the wrong piles or ignores where the rest of the deck must ultimately go.\n46. When a variant is structurally coherent but won't compile, fix exact card accounting before changing gameplay. A minimal count correction preserves the current design's roles and lets simulation reveal whether the real problem is balance, mobility, or the win sink.\n47. When a game loses with high search counts and broad pile usage, first inspect whether its supposed victory sink is truly compatible with the deck and tightly gated enough to represent progress rather than churn. In multi-copy decks, empty receiving piles should usually have a specific entry card (such as Ace or King) so they don't become accidental dead sinks.\n48. When a variant fails to compile, prefer the smallest fix that preserves the intended card flow and objectives; getting back to a runnable baseline is more valuable than speculative balance changes. Also verify which rule contexts can legally reference the draw pile, since not every pile-like keyword is valid everywhere.\n49. When a solitaire uses duplicated copies of the same suit, check foundation capacity against the real number of complete ace-to-king sequences in the deck, not against the nominal 52-card standard. A game with plenty of movement but very few wins often needs a larger or better-aligned irreversible sink more than it needs additional tableau freedom.\n50. When a deck contains duplicate suit-progressions, foundation capacity has to scale with those duplicates; otherwise the game can look mobile while being mathematically unable to absorb all cards. Before loosening tableau rules, check whether the win sink can actually hold the full deck under the deck\u2019s real composition.\n51. When a game asks the player to empty the tableau, first verify that the irreversible sink can actually absorb the entire deck under the real deck multiplicity and starting fills. A foundation system that matches a familiar pattern but has only half the needed total capacity will produce unwinnable churn no matter how much tableau mobility you add.\n52. If a receiving pile is one-way or nearly one-way, either make it the actual victory sink or give it a reliable release route; otherwise it tends to create mathematically unwinnable states. When losses are fully exhausted with low effective progress, first align the win condition with the irreversible sink already present before adding more mobility.\n53. When a solitaire shows high move counts and near-total card usage but no wins, suspect reversible churn rather than lack of mobility. Before adding more tableau freedom, make sure the irreversible sink can mathematically absorb the whole deck under the actual deck composition and foundation rule.\n54. When a solitaire prototype fails to compile, restore exact deck accounting with the smallest role-preserving adjustment before changing rules. A valid, runnable baseline is more informative than speculative balance tweaks layered onto a broken setup.\n55. When a solitaire file won't compile, fix exact card accounting before tuning rules or difficulty; even a promising move system can't be assessed until every card has a legal starting home. Prefer the smallest count correction that preserves the game's existing roles, so later playtest data reflects the design rather than a rewrite.\n56. When a special stock/draw pile isn't a legal target in win conditions, express completion through the ordinary piles that prove the stock has been fully consumed and meaningfully absorbed. Fix compile-level objective/sink mismatches before tuning difficulty; otherwise playtest data is noise.\n57. When a compact tableau already wins on many deals but some shuffles die in 0\u20133 moves, add a small amount of workspace before changing the sink or core build logic. A single empty pile can rescue opening contact and improve deal coverage without turning the game into unrestricted churn.\n58. When most losses are fully exhausted after 0\u20131 moves, fix the progress route before adding more mobility: the design usually needs a reachable sink, not more rearrangement. In reduced-deck solitaire, a conventional ace-up foundation can underfit the card set; matching the sink to the deck\u2019s actual completion pattern is often the smallest effective repair.\n59. When a solitaire already has abundant legal rearrangement but almost no wins, the next fix is usually to strengthen the irreversible progress sink, not to add even more mobility. A stepwise sink that ordinary exposed cards can enter early is often far healthier than a foundation that only accepts rare preassembled patterns.\n60. When a game shows high card usage but no wins and repeated search-cap losses, the problem is often reversible churn rather than lack of mobility. In that situation, first tighten the loosest movement route so tableau organization points toward the win sink instead of letting newly exposed cards circulate almost arbitrarily.\n61. When a game uses multiple deck copies, standard-looking ace-up foundations often need to scale in count as well as in rule. If random stock cards are dealt into non-winning one-way piles, either make those piles active workspace with exits, or move the game's true progress sink elsewhere.\n62. When a solitaire already has broad card access and a valid sink but still loses at the search cap, the problem is often churn rather than deadness. Adding a stack move that follows the tableau\u2019s existing build pattern can turn reversible shuffling into purposeful organization without making the game trivial.\n63. When a game shows high move counts and broad pile usage but zero wins, the problem is often not mobility but lack of an effective sink. In reduced-suit or duplicated-deck setups, make sure the main scoring piles accept progress at a granularity and matching rule that fits the actual card population, so ordinary exposure can become irreversible advancement.\n64. When a solitaire has lots of legal rearrangement but no wins, first check whether any irreversible sink actually matches the win condition. Turning an existing one-way receiving area into the explicit completion target often helps more than adding yet more tableau mobility, and rank-gating empty sink starts can reduce churn without removing the core route to progress.\n65. When a solitaire variant already wins sometimes but losses mostly hit search limits instead of exhausting quickly, the problem is often excessive reversible mobility rather than insufficient access. A small restriction on who may occupy empty spaces can reduce churn and increase decision quality without rewriting the core build pattern.\n66. When a solitaire generates long, non-exhaustive losses with high pile usage, the usual problem is not lack of mobility but lack of rewarded, irreversible progress. Converting an existing one-way holding area into the main victory sink is often a better fix than adding still more tableau freedom.\n67. When a solitaire file fails to compile for unused cards, fix deck accounting first with the smallest change that preserves the game\u2019s existing flow; only after the setup is runnable do win-rate and decision-quality adjustments become meaningful.\n\nIf a stock already feeds active piles in a coherent way, adding missing cards to that stock is often the least disruptive repair because it restores validity without silently changing the opening structure of every tableau pile.\n68. When a multi-deck game already has a foundation-style sink but most losses are long, non-exhaustive searches, the problem is often sink fragmentation rather than lack of movement. Consolidating progress into a shared incremental sink can reduce churn and turn more local improvements into durable advancement without rewriting the tableau.\n69. When a solitaire has high move counts and broad pile usage but no wins, the bottleneck is often sink capacity rather than mobility. In multi-copy or reduced-suit decks, adding enough parallel irreversible receiving piles can convert churn into real progress without rewriting the tableau rules.\n70. When a solitaire loses with many fully exhausted tiny searches and occasional 0-move starts, first repair opening contact before changing the whole objective: distribute a little more initial exposure and workspace so the existing move graph can actually begin. Small count-preserving changes to starting layout can reveal whether the core sink rules are viable or whether the design is blocked at a deeper level.\n71. When a solitaire uses multiple copies of a reduced suit set, standard-looking ace-up foundations may no longer have enough total capacity to absorb the deck. Before tuning mobility, check that the irreversible sink can mathematically hold every card required by the win condition.\n72. When a solitaire shows high search churn but no wins, first check whether its irreversible sink is actually compatible with the deck and objective. Broadening sink acceptance to match the deck\u2019s real structure often improves solvability more than adding extra tableau mobility.\n73. When a solitaire uses standard ace-up same-suit foundations with multiple deck copies, foundation count must scale to the deck\u2019s true completion capacity; otherwise the game can be structurally unwinnable even if plenty of moves exist.\n\nIf a solver shows lots of reversible motion but no wins, tightening the broadest tableau rule (especially unrestricted moves to empty piles) can improve decision quality better than adding still more mobility.\n74. When many deals die immediately while a few others devolve into long reversible searches, suspect a mismatch between mobility and the actual victory sink. Fix the sink first: a simpler, correctly-sized irreversible destination often improves both solvability and decision quality more than adding yet more tableau freedom.\n75. When a game has many legal rearrangements but no wins, first tighten its supposed \"progress\" piles so they behave like true irreversible sinks. A receiving pile that accepts almost any first card often becomes parking rather than progress, especially when the win condition requires other active piles to empty.\n76. When a solitaire already has lots of reversible tableau motion but no wins, the next fix is usually to add or improve an incremental, irreversible sink rather than more mobility. Requiring fully assembled runs before any card can score often creates search churn; allowing ordinary exposed cards to advance step by step turns local order into steady progress.\n77. When a game\u2019s objective is to empty active piles, check the sink capacity against the actual deck composition, not against a familiar template. A rearrangement-only tableau or too-few foundations can make every deal provably unwinnable even if the move rules themselves look reasonable.\n78. When a solitaire asks major play areas to empty, it needs a reachable one-way sink that can absorb ordinary cards incrementally under the actual deck structure. If the only special receiving piles accept too narrow a subset or allow easy backflow, most losses will be structural impossibility rather than interesting difficulty.\n79. When a solitaire prototype fails to compile for deck accounting, fix that exact arithmetic mismatch before tuning rules. A minimal count correction that preserves pile roles is usually the best next step, because it reveals the real gameplay issues without confounding them with a redesign.\n80. When a solitaire already has abundant reversible movement but never wins, first check whether its one-way receiving piles are actually part of the objective. A sink that absorbs cards without contributing directly to victory often creates search churn; aligning the win condition to that sink can convert the same move graph into meaningful progress with minimal rule changes.\n81. When a solitaire file fails to compile, fix the smallest structural inconsistency first\u2014especially exact deck accounting\u2014before tuning difficulty. A minimal repair preserves the current design intent and produces useful playtest data instead of obscuring the original idea with a larger rewrite.\n82. When a solitaire\u2019s goal is to empty working piles, the design needs a real irreversible sink that can mathematically absorb the whole deck under the actual deck composition. If a one-way receiving pile already exists, aligning victory to that pile is often the smallest effective repair.\n83. When a variant fails to compile, prefer the smallest repair that restores exact card accounting while preserving the existing move graph; only after the game runs should you judge whether its sinks and mobility are well balanced. In practice, adding missing cards to an already-active pile is often more informative than introducing new pile types or rewriting rules at the same time.\n84. When a solitaire design fails to compile, first restore exact card accounting with the smallest setup change that preserves the intended roles of the piles. A runnable but imperfect core produces far more useful feedback than a larger speculative redesign made before basic validation.\n85. When a variant hasn't even reached simulation yet, fix the smallest structural issue that restores a runnable game before tuning difficulty. Exact deck accounting is not just bookkeeping: until every card has a defined starting role, you can't learn anything trustworthy about the rules themselves.\n86. When a game has lots of explored movement but no wins, check whether the irreversible sink can mathematically absorb the whole deck under the actual suit/deck counts. A foundation system copied from a standard deck often under-capacitates duplicated suits or multiple deck copies, creating endless churn instead of progress.\n87. When a compact solitaire already wins often but some losses terminate after only a handful of fully-explored moves, the problem is usually opening contact, not late-game depth. In those cases, a small increase in initial visibility or workspace is often a better fix than broadening movement rules.\n88. When a game\u2019s objective is \u201cempty the active piles,\u201d every card outside those piles still needs a defined path into the win condition. If a stock/deal pile participates in play but is omitted from victory, the design may be mathematically unwinnable even before difficulty is considered.\n89. When a solitaire uses a reduced-suit or duplicated deck, standard foundation counts can silently under-capacity the win path. Before tuning difficulty, verify that the sink can mathematically absorb the whole deck, then align tableau rules to that deck\u2019s actual suit/color structure so ordinary openings can reach it.\n90. When a rules engine forbids duplicate source/destination move definitions, fix the conflict by merging exceptions into a single condition tree before changing the game\u2019s strategy. Restoring a runnable baseline with minimal structural disruption makes later balance signals much easier to trust.\n91. When a solitaire already has broad tableau mobility but low win rate, adding a small amount of tightly-limited workspace can help more than loosening the main build rules. The best workspace changes are capacity-aware and compensated elsewhere in the layout, so they improve decisions without turning the game into unrestricted shuffling.\n92. When a game relies on empty columns as its main workspace, make sure both single-card and stack movement can access that space under compatible conditions. If empties help only isolated cards but not already-organized sequences, some deals become dead or excessively churny even when the core build rule is sound.\n93. When a solitaire asks the player to empty all active piles, first verify that the irreversible sink can mathematically absorb the whole deck under its acceptance rule. A familiar foundation pattern that works for 52 cards in one context can become unwinnable if the game\u2019s pile counts or victory condition require more total sink capacity.\n94. When a solitaire already wins often with full card usage but shows very long solution paths or search-cap losses, the problem is often excess reversible mobility rather than lack of progress. Narrowing access to empty tableau spaces is a strong first lever because it reduces churn without changing the core sink or card flow.\n95. When a solitaire file fails deck accounting, favor the smallest count correction that preserves the intended move graph and pile roles; restoring a runnable baseline is more valuable than speculative balance changes before any play data exists.\n\nIf a deal stock feeds the main active piles, adding the missing cards there is often a cleaner repair than inflating sink piles or tableau counts, because it fixes compilation while keeping opening structure recognizable.\n96. When a game is unwinnable on most deals with many 0\u20133 move exhaustions, check whether the victory sink is mathematically compatible with the deck before tuning tableau mobility. A small sink-capacity fix that matches the actual suit/deck structure often does more than adding more rearrangement freedom.\n97. When almost all losses are fully exhausted and card usage stays near zero, the problem is often not \u201cdifficulty\u201d but missing irreversible progress. Before adding more mobility, make sure the deck has enough sink capacity to absorb all cards under the actual suit/color structure.\n98. When a solver explores deeply without winning, suspect reversible churn rather than simple lack of moves. In those cases, converting a single large all-or-nothing sink into several incremental completion sinks often improves both solvability and decision quality more than adding yet more tableau mobility.\n99. When a game shows broad pile usage and medium-length failed searches rather than immediate exhaustion, the problem is often reversible churn, not lack of legal moves. In that situation, tightening the loosest rearrangement rule and making the main sink more coherent usually helps more than adding extra mobility.\n100. When a solitaire already has a one-way accumulation area, first try aligning victory to that sink before adding more mobility. Losses with high search depth often come from progress not being properly rewarded, not from too few legal moves.\n\nProduce the next version of skill.md: rewrite it as a whole, integrating what's genuinely new or well-supported from the insights above into the existing document rather than appending to it. Keep the entire document to at most 20 bullet points total, organized under a small number of clear headers. End your response with the complete new skill.md in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```skill\n# skill.md\n\n## Establish a valid, playable core\n- Treat compile failures, bad deck accounting, invalid references, impossible predicates, overlapping move-definition conflicts, and vacuous win clauses as first-order design failures. Restore a runnable game before tuning difficulty.\n- Prefer minimal, informative repairs: preserve existing pile roles, card flow, and move graph where possible; restore exact card totals; and re-check that every rule, sink, and win-condition target is valid in that rules context.\n- Validate rule/setup compatibility early. Build and sink predicates must match the actual deck structure: reduced suits, duplicated suits, multiple deck copies, prefilled receiving piles, or collapsed color distributions can make standard-looking tableau or foundation rules impossible, trivial, or much narrower than intended.\n- Keep move definitions compact and non-overlapping. If the engine treats a source/destination pair as a single rule slot, merge exceptions into one coherent condition tree instead of splitting them across multiple moves.\n\n## Align sinks, victory, and deck structure\n- If the win condition requires active piles to empty, the design needs a reachable, irreversible sink that can mathematically absorb the entire deck under the real suit/copy structure. Rearrangement alone cannot satisfy an emptying goal.\n- Size sink capacity and parallelism to the deck\u2019s true completion structure, not to convention. Duplicated suits, repeated suit-runs, reduced-suit decks, or multiple deck copies often require different foundation counts or capacities than a standard 52-card template.\n- Match the sink rule to the deck and to ordinary play. Prefer sinks that accept progress in small, common steps; all-at-once completion gates or sinks that only fit rare preassembled patterns usually create churn instead of wins.\n- When a one-way receiving or accumulation area already exists, usually make it the main victory sink, expand it if needed, or align the win condition to it before adding new subsystems or more tableau freedom.\n- One-way piles must either represent rewarded completion or have a reliable release route. Otherwise they become dead storage, blockers, or mathematically unwinnable bookkeeping.\n- Keep main progress sinks genuinely sink-like: irreversible by default, with specific entry conditions when needed to prevent them from becoming generic parking or accidental churn routes.\n- Make sure every participating pile type has a path into the objective. If a stock, deal pile, or auxiliary area feeds play but is omitted from completion logic, the game may be structurally unwinnable.\n\n## Shape mobility to support progress\n- Distinguish deadness from churn. Near-zero use, 0\u20133 move losses, and tiny exhausted searches usually indicate missing opening contact or no reachable first progress route; high move counts, broad usage, and search-cap losses usually indicate reversible motion without enough rewarded progress.\n- When movement is plentiful but wins are absent, improve the sink before adding mobility: verify capacity, align victory to real progress, and make ordinary exposed cards able to score irreversible advancement.\n- When wins already exist but losses mostly hit search limits or solutions are very long, tighten the broadest reversible routes before adding new ones. Unrestricted empty-space fills, loose helper piles, and generic low-commitment shuffling are common churn sources.\n- Favor movement rules that organize cards in the same direction the sink rewards. Stack moves or tableau builds are best when they convert exposure into sink-ready order rather than free circulation disconnected from victory.\n- Broad holding areas are risky. Helper piles should be clearly temporary and clearable, or else be converted into structured progress zones that directly support completion.\n\n## Tune openings and workspace with small changes\n- For frequent 0- or 1-move losses in an otherwise coherent game, first improve opening contact with the smallest compatible change: slightly more starting exposure, a narrowly available workspace destination, or a modest increase in effective tableau capacity.\n- Prefer constrained empty-pile rules over unrestricted ones. Rank-gated, single-card, or otherwise narrow access to empties often rescues dead starts while preserving planning value and reducing solver noise.\n- Keep empty-space permissions internally consistent. If empty columns are intended as workspace, single-card and stack movement should be able to use them under compatible conditions; mismatched access rules create accidental dead ends and needless churn.\n- If some deals already win cleanly but others die almost immediately, suspect blocked opening information or cramped early workspace before rewriting the core build rules.\n- When a stock or deal pile feeds cards into active play, use count-preserving setup changes that preserve that flow where possible; pacing elements work best when they expose or supply cards that can realistically reach the main sink.\n```"}]